1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:17,400
Your app can fetch data. But close it, reopen it, and everything's gone. This module is
about memory — and about the one storage decision that, if you get it wrong, becomes a
genuine security problem.

2
00:00:17,400 --> 00:00:23,400
Here is what you will be able to do by the end of this video.

3
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:40,200
The key insight is that there is no single right answer. iOS gives you several mechanisms
deliberately, and the skill is matching each to the weight and sensitivity of your data. Get
that wrong and your app is either slow or insecure.

4
00:00:40,200 --> 00:00:55,000
First, UserDefaults. Key-value storage for small, lightweight things — which filter was last
selected, whether the user has seen the welcome screen. You set a value with a key and read
it back with the same key.

5
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:12,200
One rule: the type must match. Save a Bool and read it as a String and you'll get an error.
A naming habit that prevents this — prefix boolean keys with 'is' or 'has', so the key
itself reads as a yes-or-no question.

6
00:01:12,200 --> 00:01:22,200
Second, FileManager. When your data is bigger and structured, you encode it as JSON and
write it to a file in the app's Documents directory.

7
00:01:22,200 --> 00:01:35,000
This is how the reference app stores bookings, profiles and reviews. Codable turns the model
into JSON, FileManager writes it, and on next launch it reads straight back into the same
types.

8
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:49,000
Third, Core Data. This is Apple's heavyweight option — a full object graph with entities,
attributes and relationships. You'd reach for it when you have relational data, or a large
offline dataset that syncs later.

9
00:01:49,000 --> 00:02:07,400
The reference app deliberately doesn't use it. For this volume of data, JSON files were
simpler and easier to reason about. I'd flag that as a real skill: knowing when not to reach
for the heavy tool is as valuable as knowing how to use it.

10
00:02:07,400 --> 00:02:13,400
And now the fourth, which is the most important part of this module. The Keychain.

11
00:02:13,400 --> 00:02:22,200
There's a belief that circulates that UserDefaults is encrypted on iOS, so it's fine for
passwords and tokens. That is not true,

12
00:02:22,200 --> 00:02:34,200
UserDefaults writes to a plain, unencrypted file inside your app's container, with no access
control. Anyone with file system access, or an unencrypted device backup, can read it in
seconds.

13
00:02:34,200 --> 00:02:43,000
Passwords, tokens, API keys, anything personal — those go in the Keychain, which is
encrypted and protected by the operating system itself.

14
00:02:43,000 --> 00:03:01,800
Here's the real implementation. Notice the accessibility setting on this query: the 'this
device only' variant, which means the credential never travels into an iCloud or iTunes
backup. It stays on the device it was created on. Pick the most restrictive class your app

15
00:03:01,800 --> 00:03:12,200
A practical tip that'll save you debugging time: whatever text encoding you save with, you
must read back with. Mismatched encodings are a confusing Keychain failure.

16
00:03:12,200 --> 00:03:24,200
Let's see persistence working. I've confirmed a booking, and it has an ID. Now I'm going to
close the app completely — not background it, close it — and reopen.

17
00:03:24,200 --> 00:03:33,400
There it is. That came from a JSON file on disk, decoded straight back into the same Swift
types it was saved from.

18
00:03:33,400 --> 00:03:44,200
And the account. The profile details are in a JSON file. The password is in the Keychain.
Never in the same place, and never in plain text.

19
00:03:44,200 --> 00:03:46,700
Let's recap the key points.

20
00:03:46,700 --> 00:03:49,200
Here is your practice exercise.

21
00:03:49,200 --> 00:03:58,800
Module six is about structure — how to organise all of this so the app doesn't collapse
under its own weight as it grows.
